#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eigensystem

#% text
# `eigensystem(m)` gives `[eigenvalues(m), eigenvectors(m)]`, the eigenvalues and an eigenvector for each.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The two lists correspond element by element; see `eigenvectors` for repeated and defective eigenvalues.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eigensystem([[2, 1], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= [[3, 1], [[1, 1], [-1, 1]]]

#% cell
eigensystem([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[5/2 + (1/2)*33^(1/2), 5/2 - (1/2)*33^(1/2)], [[-1/2 + (1/6)*33^(1/2), 1], [-1/2 - (1/6)*33^(1/2), 1]]]

#% cell
eigensystem([[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 3.0]])
#% output
#= [[3.618033988749894, 1.3819660112501049], [[0.5257311121191336, 0.8506508083520401], [0.8506508083520401, -0.5257311121191336]]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Diagonalizing a symmetric matrix: with the eigenvectors as columns `p`, `p^-1.m.p` is diagonal:

#% cell
m = [[2, 1], [1, 2]]; p = transpose(eigensystem(m)[2]); dot(inverse(p), m, p)
#% output
#= [[3, 0], [0, 1]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `eigenvalues`, `eigenvectors`, `characteristic_polynomial`
